---
// Strand 2's synthesis page: the iamata (patients travel to the god) read
// against the Epidemics and AWP (the physician travels to the patients), on
// the map. Counts and lists are computed from iamata.json,
// epidemics-cases.json and sites.json, so they stay right as the data
// changes; the prose is placeholder or marked Claude draft.
import ScholarlyLayout from '../layouts/ScholarlyLayout.astro';
import SiteMap from '../components/SiteMap.jsx';
import sites from '../data/sites.json';
import iamata from '../data/iamata.json';
import cases from '../data/epidemics-cases.json';
import photos from '../data/photos.json';
import { originOf, taleUrl } from '../utils/iamata.js';

// Landmarks are typed by hand into epidemics-cases.json; like the corpus
// `marks`, each must be copied exactly from Jones's opening sentence.
for (const c of cases) {
  if (c.landmark && !c.opening.includes(c.landmark)) {
    throw new Error(`epidemics-cases.json: landmark "${c.landmark}" not found in ${c.ref}`);
  }
}

// Origins of the iamata petitioners, most tales first.
const origins = Object.values(
  iamata.reduce((acc, t) => {
    const place = originOf(t);
    if (!place) return acc;
    (acc[place.id] ??= { place, tales: [] }).tales.push(t);
    return acc;
  }, {})
).sort((a, b) => b.tales.length - a.tales.length || a.place.name.localeCompare(b.place.name));
const noOrigin = iamata.filter((t) => !t.origin);
const placedOrigins = origins.filter((o) => o.place.lat != null);
const unplacedOrigins = origins.filter((o) => o.place.lat == null);
const away = iamata.filter((t) => t.away);
const visitedOrigins = origins.filter((o) => o.place.visited && o.place.id !== 'epidauros');

// Epidemics case settings.
const cities = Object.entries(
  cases.reduce((acc, c) => {
    if (c.city) (acc[c.city] ??= []).push(c);
    return acc;
  }, {})
).sort((a, b) => b[1].length - a[1].length);
const named = cases.filter((c) => c.city);
const withLandmark = cases.filter((c) => c.landmark);
const caseBooks = [
  { label: 'Epid. I (after Constitution 3)', items: cases.filter((c) => c.book === 1) },
  { label: 'Epid. III, first series (before the constitution)', items: cases.filter((c) => c.series === 'first series') },
  { label: 'Epid. III, second series (after the constitution)', items: cases.filter((c) => c.series === 'second series') },
];
const read = iamata.filter((t) => t.closeReading);
---
<ScholarlyLayout title="Two Geographies of Healing">
  <p class="eyebrow">Fieldwork · Strand 2</p>
  <h1>Two Geographies of Healing [WIP]</h1>

  <div class="question-box">
    <p class="question-box__label">Question</p>
    <p>
      The Epidaurian iamata record patients who travelled to the god. The
      <em>Epidemics</em> record a physician who travelled to his patients, and
      <em>Airs, Waters, Places</em> is written for a physician arriving in a town he
      does not know. Mapped side by side, do the two kinds of text imply two different
      geographies of healing?
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: review — the question above was drafted by Claude from the Strand 2 plan.]</p>

  <nav class="page-toc" aria-label="On this page">
    <p class="page-toc__label">On this page</p>
    <ol>
      <li><a href="#summary">Summary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#map">The map</a></li>
      <li><a href="#iamata">The iamata: who came to Epidauros</a></li>
      <li><a href="#epidemics">The Epidemics: where the physician went</a></li>
      <li><a href="#awp">AWP: the physician arriving in a town</a></li>
      <li><a href="#comparison">Comparison</a></li>
      <li><a href="#fieldwork">The fieldwork</a></li>
      <li><a href="#close-readings">Close readings</a></li>
      <li><a href="#discussion">Discussion</a></li>
      <li><a href="#limitations">Limitations</a></li>
    </ol>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="summary">Summary</h2>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: a one-paragraph summary of the finding, hedged and sized to the sample, written once the comparison is done.]</p>

  <h2 id="map">The map</h2>
  <p>
    Purple lines join each petitioner’s home town to Epidauros; their thickness is the
    number of tales from that town. Teal rings are the towns where the
    <em>Epidemics</em> are set. Solid markers are the sites visited in 2026; hollow ones
    are known only from the texts. Click a marker for the tales or cases set there.
  </p>
  <SiteMap
    client:only="react"
    sites={sites}
    iamata={iamata}
    cases={cases}
    photos={photos}
    layers={['iamata', 'hippocratic', 'cult-healing']}
  />
  <p class="translation-source">
    Places and coordinates from the <a href="https://pleiades.stoa.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pleiades</a>
    gazetteer (each popup links to its entry). Uncertain identifications are noted in
    the popups.
  </p>

  <h2 id="iamata">The iamata: who came to Epidauros</h2>
  <p>
    The two best-preserved stelai (IG IV²,1 121–122) hold {iamata.length} tales:
    see the <a href="/epigraphy#iamata">catalogue</a>. {iamata.length - noOrigin.length} of
    them name where the petitioner came from, giving {origins.length} places
    ({placedOrigins.length} on the map, {unplacedOrigins.length} that cannot be placed);
    in the other {noOrigin.length} no place is named or it is lost.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="claims-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">From</th><th scope="col">Tales</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {origins.map((o) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{o.place.name}{o.place.lat == null && ' (not on the map)'}{o.place.region && ' (region)'}</td>
            <td>
              {o.tales.map((t, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={taleUrl(t)}>{t.n}. {t.petitioner}</a></>)}
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    {away.length} tales tell a story that reaches beyond Epidauros:
    {away.map((t, i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}<a href={taleUrl(t)}>{t.n}</a></>)}.
  </p>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: what the catalogue shows about distance and reach, and what it cannot show: these are tales selected for display at the sanctuary, not a register of visitors.]</p>

  <h2 id="epidemics">The <em>Epidemics</em>: where the physician went</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/sources/hippocratic-corpus/epidemics/constitution-1">Constitutions 1</a>–<a href="/sources/hippocratic-corpus/epidemics/constitution-3">3</a>
    each open “In Thasos”; <a href="/sources/hippocratic-corpus/epidemics/constitution-4">Constitution 4</a>
    names no place. Of the {cases.length} case histories in <em>Epidemics</em> I and III,
    {named.length} name a town in their opening sentence:
    {cities.map(([city, cs], i) => <>{i > 0 && ', '}{city} ({cs.length})</>)}. The others name
    at most a street, a building or a household. In all, {withLandmark.length} cases place
    the patient by such a landmark.
  </p>
  {caseBooks.map((b) => (
    <>
      <h3>{b.label}</h3>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table class="claims-table case-settings-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">Case</th><th scope="col">Town</th><th scope="col">Where in town (Jones)</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {b.items.map((c) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{c.ref.replace(/^.*Case /, 'Case ')}</td>
                <td>{c.city ?? '—'}</td>
                <td>{c.landmark ? `“${c.landmark}”` : '—'}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </>
  ))}
  <p class="translation-source">
    From the opening sentence of each case in Jones’s translation (Perseus TEI, via
    <code>scripts/case-settings.py</code>). [PLACEHOLDER: verify against the printed Loeb.]
  </p>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: what the settings show — a physician moving between households and streets of one town, and between towns of the northern Aegean — and how far the unplaced Epid. I cases can be assumed to be in Thasos (see Findings 3.4).]</p>

  <h2 id="awp"><em>Airs, Waters, Places</em>: the physician arriving in a town</h2>
  <p>
    AWP opens with a physician who travels: “on arrival at a town with which he is
    unfamiliar, a physician should examine its position with respect to the winds and to
    the risings of the sun” (AWP 1); if he knows these things, he “will not, on arrival at
    a town with which he is unfamiliar, be ignorant of the local diseases” (AWP 2).
  </p>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: discussion of AWP 1–2 once the Introduction page is annotated — the itinerant physician as the reader the treatise assumes.]</p>

  <h2 id="comparison">Comparison</h2>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: introduce the comparison; the rows follow the questions the Strand 2 plan asks of both kinds of text, with geography first.]</p>
  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="claims-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Iamata (Epidauros)</th><th scope="col"><em>Epidemics</em> I and III</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who travels</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Cause of illness</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Time scale</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Who is recorded</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">What counts as proof</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Failure</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Purpose of the record</th><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td><td>[PLACEHOLDER]</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="fieldwork">The fieldwork</h2>
  <p>
    The fieldwork gives the setting of the iamata, not evidence about them. Besides
    Epidauros itself, {visitedOrigins.length} of the places visited are named as
    petitioners’ homes:
    {visitedOrigins.map((o, i) => (
      <>{i > 0 && (i === visitedOrigins.length - 1 ? ' and ' : ', ')}<a href={o.place.journal}>{o.place.name}</a> ({o.tales.map((t) => `${t.n}. ${t.petitioner}`).join(', ')})</>
    ))}.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href="/field-journal/2026-07-25-epidaurus">Epidauros</a>: the abaton, where the stelai were displayed. [PLACEHOLDER: retrospective observation and photos.]</li>
    <li><a href="/field-journal/2026-07-26-corinth">Corinth</a>: terracotta body-part votives in the site museum, the kind of dedication the iamata describe. [PLACEHOLDER: retrospective observation and photos.]</li>
    <li><a href="/field-journal/2026-08-02-athens">Athens</a>: the Epidaurus room of the National Archaeological Museum. [PLACEHOLDER: retrospective observation and photos.]</li>
    <li><a href="/field-journal/2026-07-29-kos">Kos</a>: an Asklepieion on the island the tradition gives to Hippocrates, where both models of healing met. [PLACEHOLDER: retrospective observation and photos.]</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    {photos.length > 0
      ? `${photos.length} geotagged photographs are on the map (layer “My route, 2026”).`
      : '[PLACEHOLDER: geotagged photographs, added with scripts/photo-locations.py, will appear on the map’s route layer.]'}
  </p>

  <h2 id="close-readings">Close readings</h2>
  {read.length > 0 ? (
    <ul>{read.map((t) => <li><a href={taleUrl(t)}>{t.n}. {t.petitioner}</a>: {t.complaint}</li>)}</ul>
  ) : (
    <p>[PLACEHOLDER: 5–6 tales read closely, 2–3 in the author’s own translation; see the <a href="/epigraphy#iamata">catalogue</a>.]</p>
  )}

  <h2 id="discussion">Discussion</h2>
  <p>Leads to test (not conclusions):</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href={taleUrl(iamata[21])}>Hermon of Thasos (22)</a> came to Epidauros from the town
      where the physician of Constitutions 1–3 worked.
    </li>
    <li>
      In <a href={taleUrl(iamata[32])}>33</a> the cure follows Thersandros home to Halieis, and
      Halieis founds a precinct of Asklepios: the cult travels, not the patient. In
      <a href={taleUrl(iamata[22])}>23</a> Aristagora incubates at Troizen while the god is
      away at Epidauros.
    </li>
    <li>
      Tales <a href={taleUrl(iamata[20])}>21</a> and <a href={taleUrl(iamata[23])}>24</a> have a
      parent incubate on a child’s behalf, so the patient does not travel at all.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: review — the leads above were drafted by Claude from the catalogue. Then the discussion itself.]</p>

  <h2 id="limitations">Limitations</h2>
  <p>[PLACEHOLDER: limitations — e.g. the catalogue covers two of the four stelai; origins are as the stones state them; several places are uncertain or only regions; the tales were chosen for display by the sanctuary; the Epidemics settings come from one translation.]</p>
</ScholarlyLayout>
